# Git decides text-vs-binary by scanning for a NUL byte, and small PDFs can
# easily have none. Four tracked PDFs are classified as text today, all
# encryption fixtures: crypt/base.pdf, enc_aesv2_cm.pdf, enc_rc4_40.pdf and
# enc_rc4_40_pw.pdf. On a Windows checkout (Git for Windows defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true) git rewrites every LF in them as CRLF, which shifts every
# xref offset and, where an LF lands inside ciphertext, breaks decryption
# outright. That surfaced the first time the Windows CI job got as far as
# running tests: enc_rc4_40.pdf stopped decrypting there and nowhere else.
#
# Which files fall on which side of the heuristic depends on their bytes, so it
# changes whenever a fixture is regenerated. Mark the binary types explicitly
# instead of relying on the guess.
*.pdf binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pfb binary
*.cff binary
*.icc binary

# Shell scripts must keep LF regardless of the platform they are checked out
# on, or a Windows clone produces scripts no shell will run.
*.sh text eol=lf
